.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
.TH GWPROXY 1 "July 2026" "gwproxy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gwproxy \- fast multi-threaded TCP, SOCKS5, HTTP and HTTPS proxy for GNU/Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gwproxy
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gwproxy
(the GNU/Weeb Proxy) is a fast, multi-threaded TCP proxy for GNU/Linux. A
single instance can act as any combination of:
.IP \(bu 3
a plain TCP\-to\-TCP forwarder to a fixed
.BR \-\-target ;
.IP \(bu 3
a
.B SOCKS5
proxy (RFC 1928) with CONNECT to IPv4, IPv6 and domain\-name targets, UDP
ASSOCIATE relaying to IPv4/IPv6 targets (domain\-name targets and upstream\-proxy
chaining are not supported for UDP), and optional username/password
authentication (RFC 1929);
.IP \(bu 3
an
.B HTTP
proxy supporting both CONNECT tunnelling and forwarding of ordinary
absolute\-form requests ("GET http://host/path"), with optional "Basic" proxy
authentication (RFC 7617);
.IP \(bu 3
an
.B HTTPS
proxy that terminates the client's TLS on the listener (see
.B TLS TERMINATION
below), when built with OpenSSL support;
.IP \(bu 3
a
.B transparent
proxy that recovers each connection's original destination from
.BR SO_ORIGINAL_DST .
.PP
SOCKS5 and HTTP can be enabled together on one listening port; the protocol is
auto\-detected per connection from the client's first bytes, and both share a
single credential store. When TLS termination is enabled, a TLS ClientHello is
likewise auto\-detected, so plaintext and TLS clients coexist on the same port.
.PP
gwproxy runs a configurable number of worker threads that each accept
connections through
.B SO_REUSEPORT
and drive I/O with either the
.B epoll
(default) or
.B io_uring
event loop. It recovers gracefully from file\-descriptor exhaustion
(EMFILE/ENFILE): a worker out of descriptors stops accepting and tries again
after 100 ms on io_uring; on epoll, once several connections' worth have been
closed, or after 5 seconds at the latest. It caches DNS answers for hostname
targets, tries and races the
addresses of a multi\-homed name on either loop (see
.BR "TARGET ADDRESS SELECTION" ),
and can mark its own outgoing connections with
.B SO_MARK
and pin them to a chosen source address or network interface.
.SH OPTIONS
Every boolean option takes an explicit
.B 0
or
.B 1
argument. A numeric argument must be a whole decimal integer; anything else,
or a value out of the option's range, is rejected at startup.
Long options and their single\-letter equivalents are interchangeable.
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-event\-loop=\fIname\fR
Event loop to use:
.B epoll
or
.BR io_uring .
The
.B io_uring
loop is only available when gwproxy was built with io_uring support (see
.BR "AVAILABILITY" ).
Default:
.BR epoll .
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bind=\fIaddr:port\fR
Address and port to listen on. IPv6 literals are written in brackets, e.g.
.BR [::1]:1080 " or " 0.0.0.0:8080 .
A port outside 0 to 65535, or one with anything behind its digits, is refused
at startup, here and in every other address option.
The default,
.BR [::]:1080 ,
listens on the dual\-stack IPv4/IPv6 wildcard. A SOCKS5 or HTTP proxy
listening on anything but loopback without
.B \-\-auth\-file
is open to every client that can reach it; gwproxy logs a warning at startup
when it is configured that way.
.IP
A target that is this listener is refused (SOCKS5 reply
.BR 0x01 ,
HTTP
.BR 502 ,
and a plain or transparent client is disconnected), since a client could
otherwise chain the proxy through itself over and over on one connection. That
covers a hostname, a
.B \-j DNAT
rewrite and an upstream proxy too, and an unspecified target as the address a
connect to it reaches: loopback, or an IPv4 source forced by
.B \-\-bind\-source
or
.BR "\-j BIND" .
The other addresses of a hostname are still tried, so the client may get through
to one of them, and when all fail the reply is that of the last to fail, which can
be another error code.
Under a wildcard address it means any address the host's routing table has as
local, local routes included, in the family the wildcard covers, with this port;
a service of the same port number on an address the listener does not cover is
reached as usual. Asking the routing table takes a descriptor, so while the
descriptor table is full a SOCKS5 or HTTP target on this port number is refused
as if it were this listener, even if it is a remote service; plain and transparent
clients wait for a descriptor, as they do for their target socket.
With port 0, where each worker has a port of its own,
only the worker's own port is recognised. A path back to the listener through NAT
or another host is not recognised either; use
.B \-\-max\-conns\-per\-ip
to bound what one client can do that way, noting that such connections count
against the proxy's own address.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target=\fIaddr:port\fR
For plain TCP forwarding, the fixed destination every accepted connection is
proxied to. Ignored in SOCKS5/HTTP/transparent modes, where the destination
comes from the protocol or the socket.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-nr\-workers=\fInr\fR
Number of worker threads, each with its own
.B SO_REUSEPORT
listener, from 1 to 1024. Default:
.BR 4 .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-protocol\-timeout=\fIsec\fR
Seconds allowed for a client to complete the SOCKS5/HTTP (and, if enabled, TLS)
handshake before the connection is dropped. This includes the lookup of the
target's name; a client whose request is waiting for one when the time runs out
gets a SOCKS5 reply 0x06 or an HTTP 504 first. Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-connect\-timeout=\fIsec\fR
Seconds allowed to establish the connection to the target (or upstream proxy)
before giving up. This bounds the whole attempt, including every address tried
for the same name (see
.BR "TARGET ADDRESS SELECTION" ).
.B 0
(or a negative value) sets no limit: the kernel still gives up on a target that
never answers after its SYN retries, but an upstream proxy that accepts and then
says nothing is waited for as long as the client stays. A client that resets its
connection ends the wait at once, though with io_uring on a kernel older than
5.13 only when the connect ends. One that has sent a FIN counts as staying, as
it may only have finished sending and be waiting for the answer.
Default:
.BR 5 .
.TP
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout=\fIsec\fR
Close an established tunnel, or a SOCKS5 UDP association, once no data has
moved in either direction for this many seconds. Any traffic restarts the
count, so a tunnel that keeps moving data is never closed, however long it
lives; a UDP association counts as active while datagrams go either way.
The check runs on a coarse per\-worker clock, so a pair can outlive the timeout
by up to about a second.
.B 0
(or a negative value) never closes an idle connection, which keeps long quiet
tunnels such as SSH sessions working. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-connect\-attempt\-delay=\fIms\fR
Milliseconds to wait before racing the next address of a multi\-homed target,
as in Happy Eyeballs (RFC 8305 Section 5). When a name resolves to several
addresses gwproxy starts the first, and if it has neither succeeded nor failed
within this delay it starts the next one alongside it; the first connection to
succeed wins and the others are dropped. This is what keeps a black\-holed
address cheap: without racing such an address costs the whole
.B \-\-connect\-timeout
before anything else is tried, because nothing ever reports a failure.
.B 0
or negative disables racing, leaving only sequential fallback, which still
steps to the next address when one actively fails (refused, unreachable).
Ignored when an upstream proxy is configured, since every attempt would dial
the same proxy. Which addresses are tried, in what order and how many, and how
racing behaves on each event loop, are described under
.BR "TARGET ADDRESS SELECTION" .
Default:
.BR 250 .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid\-file=\fIfile\fR
Write the process ID to
.IR file ,
which is locked while the proxy runs and removed when it exits. A symlink is
not followed. A file that cannot be written (a symlink, one locked by another
instance, a directory that cannot be written to) is warned about, and the
proxy runs without it.
Not written by default.
.SS Proxy mode selection
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-as\-socks5=\fI0|1\fR
Run as a SOCKS5 proxy. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-as\-http=\fI0|1\fR
Run as an HTTP proxy (CONNECT and forwarding). Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-udp\-associate=\fI0|1\fR
Allow the SOCKS5
.B UDP ASSOCIATE
command. When
.BR 0 ,
the proxy rejects it with reply
.B 0x07
(command not supported), leaving TCP
.B CONNECT
unaffected; only meaningful with
.BR \-\-as\-socks5 .
Datagrams are taken only from the IP address of the control connection. When
the request declares that address and a port, only that port is the client;
when it declares any other address, or no port, the first valid datagram from
that IP address decides the port, so another party on the same IP address can
get there first. A client behind a NAT cannot know its outside port and is
served by the latter rule. A client that declares the control connection's
address with a port the NAT rewrites gets no service.
Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-as\-transparent=\fI0|1\fR
Run as a transparent proxy, taking the target from
.B SO_ORIGINAL_DST
(see
.BR "TRANSPARENT PROXY" ).
Cannot be combined with
.B \-\-as\-socks5
or
.BR \-\-as\-http .
Default:
.BR 0 .
.PP
.RS
If none of the above is set and
.B \-\-target
is given, gwproxy is a plain TCP forwarder.
.B \-\-as\-socks5
and
.B \-\-as\-http
may be set together to serve both protocols on one port.
.RE
.SS Authentication
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-auth\-file=\fIfile\fR
File of
.B username:password
credentials, one per line, shared by SOCKS5 (RFC 1929) and HTTP "Basic" (RFC
7617) authentication. When set, clients of either protocol must present valid
credentials. The file is reloaded on
.BR SIGHUP ,
and also on change with
.BR \-\-watch\-files=1 .
It must be a regular file: anything else (a FIFO, a device) is refused at
startup, and a reload that finds one keeps the current credentials (see
.BR "RELOADING THE FILES" ).
Without it, no authentication is required (see
.BR "AUTHENTICATION" ).
.TP
.BR \-\-watch\-files=\fI0|1\fR
Also reload
.B \-\-auth\-file
and the
.B \-\-acl\-file
set by themselves when they change, in addition to
.B SIGHUP
(default: 0). With 0, the files are read at startup and then only when
.B SIGHUP
asks for it: nothing watches them, so editing a file has no effect until the
signal. See
.BR "RELOADING THE FILES" .
.SS Access control
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-acl\-file=\fIfile\fR
Load an iptables\-style access\-control list that filters incoming clients
.RB ( INPUT " chain)"
and outgoing targets
.RB ( OUTPUT " chain)."
The file is reloaded on
.BR SIGHUP ,
and also on change with
.B \-\-watch\-files=1
(see
.BR "RELOADING THE FILES" ).
A malformed file is rejected (at startup, or by keeping the previous rules on
reload); so is an empty file, one with a NUL byte, one that changes while it is
read, and anything that is not a regular file (a FIFO is refused, not waited
on). Under
.BR \-\-watch\-files=1 ,
a file that is rewritten in place can still be reloaded half written; replace it
atomically instead: write the new rules to a temporary file in the same
directory and rename it over the old one. When
.B \-\-acl\-file
is not given, a built\-in default ACL is applied instead (see
.BR \-\-acl\-allow\-all
below). Each line is one directive, evaluated top\-to\-bottom
with first\-match\-wins; a chain's policy applies when nothing matches.
A word that starts with
.B #
begins a comment that runs to the end of the line; a
.B #
inside a word (a regexp such as
.BR ^[^#]+$ )
is part of that word. The directives are:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.BI "\-P " "chain target"
\(em set a chain's default policy
.RB ( ACCEPT " or " REJECT ).
.IP \(bu 3
.BI "include " path ;
\(em read the rules of another file in place of this line, so first\-match
order is the order of the lines as read top to bottom through every file. A
relative
.I path
is taken from the directory of the file that holds the line, an absolute one as
given; it is one word (no quoting, no globs), and the
.B ;
is required (it may also stand apart as the last word). An included file may
include others, up to 8 levels below the
.B \-\-acl\-file
(a chain of 9 files), one load reads at most 64 files (the
.B \-\-acl\-file
and every include counted, a file included twice twice), and none may include
itself, directly or through others. Every file of the set, the
.B \-\-acl\-file
included, must be a regular file (a FIFO or a device is refused rather than
waited on) and is held to the same checks (not empty, no NUL byte, not changed
while read, no malformed line), and
the set loads whole or not at all: any failure refuses it at startup, or keeps
the previous rules on reload.
.B SIGHUP
reloads the whole set. With
.BR \-\-watch\-files=1 ,
every file of the set is watched like the
.B \-\-acl\-file
itself, and a change to any of them reloads the whole set. The watched files
follow the include lines: a file named by a new line is watched from that
reload on, even while it is still missing (create it and the set reloads), and
one whose line is gone no longer is.
.IP \(bu 3
.BI "upstream " name " {"
\(em define an upstream pool, a set of upstream proxies that
.B "\-j UPSTREAM"
rules send connections through (see
.BR "UPSTREAM CHAINING" ).
The line stands alone, and so does the
.B }
that ends the block; each line between holds one directive ending in
.BR ; :
.BI "server " url ;
adds a server, with a URL as
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
takes it
.RB ( socks5:// ", " socks5h:// ", " http:// " or " https:// ,
optional
.BR user:pass@ ,
a name, an IPv4 address or a bracketed IPv6 one, an optional port), and
.B least_conn;
(at most once) sets the method; round\-robin is the default.
.B https://
servers need a build with OpenSSL support; without it they fail the load.
A pool has 1 to 8192 servers (one more fails the load), and all of them must
resolve target names the same way:
.B socks5://
servers have gwproxy resolve them,
.BR socks5h:// ", " http:// " and " https://
servers resolve them themselves, and a pool mixing the two is refused. A
.I name
is 1 to 64 letters, digits,
.BR _ ", " \- " and " . ,
.B DIRECT
is reserved, and a name is one pool across all the files of the set; a set
has at most 1024 pools. Blocks do not nest, an include line may not stand in
one, and a file may not end inside one. A pool may be defined after the rules
that use it, or in another file of the set; a rule naming a pool that is not
defined anywhere is an error. Every server is parsed and its host resolved
when the set loads (and only then: a reload resolves it again), as
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
is at startup. Host names are resolved one by one, so a large pool of names
loads as slowly as all its lookups in a row; an address needs no lookup. A host that is neither a name nor an address, a name that does
not resolve, an address
.B \-\-outgoing\-family
forbids, or a
.B \-\-target\-buf\-size
below the 1280 bytes an upstream handshake needs fails the load like any
malformed line. A file that holds a server URL with credentials is warned
about (once per load, not an error) when its group or others may read it. No
message shows the credentials: they name the pool and the server's number,
counted from 1, and show its URL without the
.B user:pass@
part.
.IP \(bu 3
.BI "\-A " "chain [matches] " "\fB\-j\fR " target
\(em append a rule. Matches, each negatable with a leading
.BR ! :
.BR \-s / \-\-source " and " \-d / \-\-destination " (" IP/CIDR ", "
.B \-d
is
.BR OUTPUT "-only), " "\-m domain \-\-domain" " (" OUTPUT "-only, target"
hostname; or
.B \-\-domain\-regexp
for a PCRE match, see below),
.BR "\-m user \-\-user" " (" OUTPUT "-only, the authenticated username; or"
.B \-\-user\-regexp
for a PCRE match),
.BR \-p / \-\-protocol " (" tcp / udp "), " \-\-sports " and " \-\-dports
(a port, a
.IR lo \- hi
range, or a comma list;
.B \-\-dports
is
.BR OUTPUT "-only). The target is " ACCEPT ", " REJECT ", " DNAT
.RB ( OUTPUT "-only) with " \-\-to\-destination " " \fIaddr\fR [ :\fIport\fR ],
which rewrites the destination (address and/or port, either optional) and, like
iptables, is terminal \(em it accepts the connection and stops matching (DNAT
applies to SOCKS5, HTTP, plain and transparent forwarding and, when chaining, to
the destination handed to an upstream proxy \(em including replacing a
.B socks5h://
hostname with the rewritten IP, or for a port\-only
.B \-\-to :\fIport\fR
keeping the hostname and changing its port \(em but not the UDP relay),
.B MARK
.RB ( OUTPUT "-only) with " \-\-set\-mark " " \fInum\fR ,
.B BIND
(below), or
.B UPSTREAM
.RB ( OUTPUT "-only) with " \-\-to " " \fIname\fR
(or its alias
.BR \-\-to\-proxy ),
which sends the connection through the upstream pool
.IR name ,
or
.BR "\-\-to DIRECT" ,
which sends it to its target directly, past any
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy .
Like
.BR MARK " and " BIND ,
.B UPSTREAM
is a composable modifier: the last matching rule's choice wins, and
.B \-\-accept
makes a rule terminal. The choice is made once per connection, before its
target's name is resolved (see
.BR "UPSTREAM CHAINING" ),
so a
.B \-d
in such a rule matches only a target the client gave as an address, never one
it named; a load warns about such a rule. It applies to TCP only: UDP never
goes through an upstream, so a rule with
.B "\-p udp"
or
.B "! \-p tcp"
could never take effect, and is a load error.
.IP
.B \-j MARK
sets the fwmark
.RB ( SO_MARK )
on the outgoing target socket, overriding the global
.BR \-\-mark ,
for policy routing / iptables matching. Unlike iptables it is a
.I composable modifier
here, not a terminal action: it records the mark and eval keeps matching, so a
connection can accumulate a mark and still fall through to a terminal
.BR ACCEPT / REJECT / DNAT
or the chain policy. The last matching
.B \-j MARK
wins. Add
.B \-\-accept
(see below) to make a single rule set the mark
.I and
terminate. Like
.BR "\-j BIND" ,
it is
.I strict:
if setting
.B SO_MARK
fails (it needs
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
or, since Linux 5.11,
.BR CAP_NET_RAW )
the connection is dropped rather than egressing unmarked via the wrong route.
It applies to TCP target connections (SOCKS5, HTTP, plain and transparent), not
the UDP relay. (The coarse global
.B \-\-mark
remains best\-effort.)
.IP
.B \-j BIND
.RB ( OUTPUT "-only) with " \-\-to\-source " " \fIaddr\fR [ :\fIport\fR ] " and/or"
.B \-\-to\-iface
.IR ifname ,
pins the outgoing connection to a source address (and/or port) and/or a network
interface \(em e.g. to route a given user's egress out of a specific WireGuard
tunnel. At least one of
.B \-\-to\-source
/
.B \-\-to\-iface
is required. Like
.BR MARK ,
it is a composable modifier (the last match wins), takes
.B \-\-accept
the same way, and applies to the same TCP target connections. It
.I replaces
the global
.BR \-\-bind\-source / \-\-bind\-iface
default wholesale for the connections it matches, rather than merging with it:
a rule carrying only
.B \-\-to\-iface
leaves the connection on its default source address, it does not inherit
.BR \-\-bind\-source .
It is
.I strict:
if binding the interface
.RB ( SO_BINDTODEVICE )
or the source address
.RB ( bind (2))
fails, the connection is dropped rather than continued on the wrong source. A
.B \-\-to\-source
whose address family does not match the address being dialled \(em the target,
or the upstream proxy when chaining \(em is one such failure; when a hostname
target resolved to addresses of both families, that candidate is skipped
instead and the connection proceeds on an address of the matching family (see
.BR "TARGET ADDRESS SELECTION" ).
Neither pinning needs a capability of its own:
.B SO_BINDTODEVICE
has been unprivileged since Linux 5.7, and binding a source address that is
configured locally never required one.
With no source
.I port
(the default) the kernel picks one at
.BR connect (2)
.RB ( IP_BIND_ADDRESS_NO_PORT ),
so each destination has the whole local port range to itself.
Binding a fixed source
.I port
limits that source to one connection at a time, whatever the destination, and
for as long as the closed one lingers in TIME_WAIT; it also defeats
multi\-address racing and fallback, since two attempts of one connection cannot
share a source port.
.RE
.IP
.B \-\-accept
.IP
Valid only on
.BR "\-j MARK" ", " "\-j BIND" " and " "\-j UPSTREAM" ,
and takes no argument: apply the modifier and then stop traversing the chain,
accepting the connection. It turns a composable modifier into a terminal action,
so
.RS
.IP
.EX
\-A OUTPUT \-d 1.2.3.4/32 \-j BIND \-\-to\-source 192.168.1.2 \-\-accept
.EE
.RE
.IP
does the work of a
.B \-j BIND
rule followed by an identically matched
.BR "\-j ACCEPT" ,
in one rule and one match. That is the point: a chain that would otherwise test
the same condition twice tests it once. A rule that does not match is unaffected
\(em
.B \-\-accept
only fires when the rule fires.
.IP
It is rejected on
.BR ACCEPT " and " REJECT ,
where it is respectively redundant and self\-contradictory, and on
.BR DNAT ,
which already terminates. Its position among a rule's options does not matter.
.IP
Addresses match IPv4 and its IPv4\-mapped IPv6 form equally. A rejected target
is refused (SOCKS5 reply
.BR 0x02 ,
HTTP
.BR 403 );
a rejected client connection is closed. For a hostname target the
.B OUTPUT
chain is evaluated once per resolved address, so a
.B \-d
match can deny some of a name's addresses while the connection still succeeds
on another (see
.BR "TARGET ADDRESS SELECTION" ).
.IP
One trailing dot does not count, in a
.B \-\-domain
value or in the requested name: "example.com." and "example.com" are the same
name. A value that still ends in a dot without it, or is nothing but one, is a
parse error. A regexp sees the requested name with its dot already removed.
.IP
.B \-\-domain\-regexp
.I pattern
(an alternative to
.BR \-\-domain ,
also under
.BR "\-m domain" )
matches the requested hostname with a PCRE. It requires a build with PCRE2
support (see
.BR "AVAILABILITY" )
and is rejected otherwise. The pattern is matched
.I raw
and unanchored (case\-insensitively): it can match anywhere in the hostname
unless you anchor it yourself with
.BR ^ " and " $ .
A pattern must contain no whitespace (rules are whitespace\-tokenised).
.IP
.BR "\-m user \-\-user" " " \fIname\fR
matches the username the client authenticated as (via
.BR \-\-auth\-file );
.B \-\-user\-regexp
takes a PCRE instead (case\-sensitive, raw/unanchored, needs
.BR \-\-use\-pcre ).
It is
.B OUTPUT
\-only. A connection with no authenticated user has no name to match, so
.B \-\-user
does not match it, and the negated
.B ! \-\-user
.I does
match it, the same way
.B ! \-\-domain
matches a target with no hostname. So
.B "\-m user ! \-\-user alice \-j ACCEPT"
admits anonymous clients too, unless every client has to authenticate. That
holds for SOCKS5 and HTTP with
.B \-\-auth\-file
only: no connection in plain or transparent mode has a user, even with
.BR \-\-auth\-file ,
and the same goes for SOCKS5 and HTTP without it.
The
.BR \-\-user\-regexp
option, like
.BR \-\-domain\-regexp ,
requires a PCRE2 build.
.IP
.BR "\-m statistic \-\-mode nth \-\-every" " " \fIn\fR " [" \-\-packet " " \fIp\fR ]
matches one evaluation in every
.IR n ,
the one whose index modulo
.I n
equals
.I p
(default 0), and is how several rules are made to share traffic between them.
.I p
must be less than
.IR n .
Only
.B nth
mode exists so far;
.B random
is rejected rather than approximated. The match may be negated
.RB ( "! \-\-every" ),
which selects the other
.IR n \-1
in
.IR n .
The negation belongs to
.B \-\-every
alone: a
.B !
before
.B \-\-mode
or
.B \-\-packet
is rejected rather than passed on to the option after it.
The match composes with every other criterion \(em the counter advances only
for connections the rest of the rule already matched, so
.B "\-d 10.0.0.0/8 \-m statistic \-\-mode nth \-\-every 2"
splits that prefix's traffic and leaves everything else alone.
.IP
Splitting
.I k
ways needs descending counts, not
.I k
equal ones: a rule is only consulted for what the rules above it passed on. For
an even three-way split write
.BR "\-\-every 3" ,
then
.BR "\-\-every 2" ,
then no statistic match at all:
.IP
.EX
\-A OUTPUT \-m statistic \-\-mode nth \-\-every 3 \-j BIND \-\-to\-source 10.0.0.1 \-\-accept
\-A OUTPUT \-m statistic \-\-mode nth \-\-every 2 \-j BIND \-\-to\-source 10.0.0.2 \-\-accept
\-A OUTPUT \-j BIND \-\-to\-source 10.0.0.3 \-\-accept
.EE
.IP
Three rules all saying
.B "\-\-every 3"
would instead give 1/3, 2/9 and 4/9, because the second only ever sees the two
thirds the first declined.
.IP
Two properties worth knowing. The counter counts evaluations rather than
connections, and the
.B OUTPUT
chain runs once per candidate address, so a target that resolves to several
addresses is counted more than once \(em pin the split to literal addresses, or
accept that it is per attempt. And the counters live in the rules, so reloading
the ACL file restarts every split from zero.
.TP
.B \-\-acl\-allow\-all
Do not apply the built\-in default ACL; allow all targets (the behaviour before
a default ACL existed). This is ignored when
.B \-\-acl\-file
is given \(em the file's rules always apply as written.
.PP
.RS
When neither
.B \-\-acl\-file
nor
.B \-\-acl\-allow\-all
is given, gwproxy applies a built\-in default ACL (SSRF hardening) that
accepts all clients but rejects outgoing connections to anything other than
public unicast addresses: the unspecified addresses (on Linux a connection to
0.0.0.0 or :: reaches the local host), loopback, private and shared (CGNAT)
space, link\-local (including the cloud metadata service at 169.254.169.254),
benchmarking, documentation, multicast, reserved and broadcast addresses, the
old 6to4 relay anycast, the IPv6 prefixes that embed an IPv4 address
(IPv4\-compatible, SIIT, NAT64, 6to4, Teredo), ORCHID, and all of IPv6 outside
2000::/3, the only part allocated for global unicast. An IPv4\-mapped target
(::ffff:a.b.c.d) is matched as the IPv4 address it carries, so the IPv4 rules
cover it too. A hostname is checked per resolved address, so a name such as
.B localhost
is refused for both its 127.0.0.1 and its ::1 address. With a remote\-DNS
upstream no address is known here, so the name itself is refused; the regexp
rule is only there in a PCRE2 build:
.RS
.nf
\-P INPUT ACCEPT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 0.0.0.0/8 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 10.0.0.0/8 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 100.64.0.0/10 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 127.0.0.0/8 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 169.254.0.0/16 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 172.16.0.0/12 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 192.0.0.0/24 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 192.0.2.0/24 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 192.88.99.0/24 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 192.168.0.0/16 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 198.18.0.0/15 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 198.51.100.0/24 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 203.0.113.0/24 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 224.0.0.0/4 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 240.0.0.0/4 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d ::/128 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d ::1/128 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d ::/96 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 64:ff9b::/96 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 64:ff9b:1::/48 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d ::/8 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 100::/8 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 200::/7 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 400::/6 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 800::/5 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 1000::/4 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 2001::/32 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 2001:2::/48 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 2001:10::/28 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 2001:db8::/32 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 2002::/16 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 3fff::/20 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 4000::/3 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 6000::/3 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 8000::/3 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d a000::/3 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d c000::/3 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d e000::/4 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d f000::/5 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d f800::/6 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d fc00::/7 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d fe00::/9 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d fe80::/10 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d fec0::/10 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d ff00::/8 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-m domain \-\-domain localhost \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-m domain \-\-domain\-regexp \e.localhost$ \-j REJECT
\-P OUTPUT ACCEPT
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-conns\-per\-ip=\fInr\fR
Hold at most
.I nr
client connections at once from one client, counted across all worker threads
and every proxy mode. An IPv4 client, and an IPv4\-mapped IPv6 one
(::ffff:a.b.c.d, as a dual\-stack listener sees IPv4 clients), is one client per
address; a native IPv6 client is one client per /64 prefix, since a single
IPv6 host normally owns a whole /64 and could otherwise pick a new address for
each connection. A connection over the cap is closed as soon as it is accepted,
before anything is read from it, and logged as a warning with the client's
address, at most once a second per client, with the number refused since the
previous line. A client that the
.B INPUT
chain rejects is not counted. A connection counts until it is closed, for any
reason, which makes room for the next one.
.B 0
means no cap. Default:
.BR 0 .
.SS TLS termination (HTTPS proxy)
These options require a build with OpenSSL support (see
.BR "AVAILABILITY" ).
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-tls\-cert=\fIfile\fR
PEM certificate chain presented to TLS clients. Providing it (together with
.BR \-\-tls\-key )
enables TLS termination on the listener. It needs
.B \-\-as\-socks5=1
or
.BR \-\-as\-http=1 :
with plain forwarding or
.B \-\-as\-transparent=1
the proxy refuses to start, as they do not terminate TLS.
.TP
.BR \-Y ", " \-\-tls\-key=\fIfile\fR
PEM private key matching
.BR \-\-tls\-cert .
Both must be given together.
.SS DNS resolution
.TP
.BR \-Q ", " \-\-prefer\-ipv6=\fI0|1\fR
Choose which address family leads when a proxy hostname target resolves to
both. With the system resolver the candidate list always interleaves the two
families and this only picks which one starts it: the default
.B 0
tries an IPv4 address first and the first IPv6 address second,
.B 1
reverses that order. Neither value suppresses a family or narrows the query,
and while racing is enabled the preference is worth at most one
.B \-\-connect\-attempt\-delay
head start (see
.BR "TARGET ADDRESS SELECTION" ).
The built\-in resolver of
.B \-\-raw\-dns
instead asks for one record type at a time, so there this selects which is
queried first \(em A, falling back to AAAA, or the reverse with
.BR 1 .
Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-\-outgoing\-family=\fIany|ipv4|ipv6\fR
Restrict outgoing (target) connections to one address family. Unlike
.BR \-\-prefer\-ipv6 ,
which only reorders candidates, this is a hard restriction with two effects:
proxy DNS resolves that family only, and a literal target of the other family
is refused before any socket is created \(em SOCKS5 reply
.B 0x08
.RB ( "address type not supported" ),
HTTP
.BR 502 .
Relayed UDP datagrams to a target of the wrong family are dropped. Default:
.BR any .
.IP
The restriction is enforced where the outgoing socket is created, which is
after the ACL has run, so a
.B \-j DNAT
rule that rewrites a destination into the forbidden family is caught as well.
It also covers the hop to an
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy ,
so an upstream whose own address is of the forbidden family is rejected at
startup rather than failing on every connection, and to the destination named
to the upstream: an address of the forbidden family, requested by the client
or made by a
.B \-j DNAT
rule, is refused the same way, before the upstream is contacted. Likewise
.B \-\-prefer\-ipv6=1
with
.BR \-\-outgoing\-family=ipv4 ,
and a
.B \-\-bind\-source
of the forbidden family, are refused at startup as contradictions.
.IP
.B Pitfalls.
This controls what
.I this
proxy dials; it is not a guarantee that no IPv6 is used end to end.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
With
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy=socks5h://
or
.BR http:// ,
the hostname is handed to the upstream and that proxy resolves it. gwproxy
never sees the address, so the upstream may well connect over IPv6. For a
hostname, only the hop to the upstream itself is restricted. With
.B socks5://
the name is resolved locally, so the restriction does apply.
.IP \(bu 2
A domain target in a relayed UDP datagram is dropped for unrelated reasons
(no per\-datagram DNS), so the family restriction never sees it.
.IP \(bu 2
It does not touch the listener. Use
.B \-\-bind\-ipv6\-only
to control which families may reach the proxy; the two are independent.
.IP \(bu 2
A name with only AAAA records becomes unresolvable under
.BR ipv4 ,
and the client is told the host could not be reached. That is the point, but
it turns a working destination into a failing one, so expect it.
.IP \(bu 2
NAT64/DNS64 networks synthesise AAAA for IPv4\-only destinations.
.B ipv4
there refuses the synthesised address and leaves no route to the destination
at all.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-\-bind\-ipv6\-only=\fI0|1\fR
Set
.B IPV6_V6ONLY
on the listening socket. With the default
.BR 0 ,
a wildcard
.B [::]
bind also accepts IPv4 clients, which appear as
.RI ::ffff: a.b.c.d
(an ACL
.B \-s 10.0.0.0/8
still matches such a client, since addresses are compared in their canonical
form). With
.BR 1 ,
those connections are refused and only IPv6 clients can reach the proxy.
.IP
The value is applied explicitly in both directions rather than only when the
option is given, so behaviour does not depend on the host's
.B net.ipv6.bindv6only
sysctl. It has no effect when
.B \-\-bind
names an IPv4 address. It applies to the listener only: the SOCKS5 UDP relay
socket stays dual\-stack on purpose, because a single\-family bind cannot
egress the other family, so UDP ASSOCIATE to IPv4 targets keeps working. On a
kernel without IPv6 (booted with
.BR ipv6.disable=1 )
the relay socket is IPv4 only and datagrams to IPv6 targets are dropped.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-dns\-cache\-secs=\fIsec\fR
Cache proxy DNS answers for this many seconds.
.B 0
or negative disables caching. Default:
.BR 0 .
The cached IP is still re-checked against the ACL
.RB ( OUTPUT
chain) on every connection. Note the cache uses this fixed duration, not the
authoritative record TTL, so a short\-TTL name may be served stale for up to
.I sec
seconds.
.TP
.BR \-\-dns\-cache\-max\-entries=\fInr\fR
Bound the DNS cache to at most
.I nr
entries; once full, expired entries are reclaimed to make room, and with none
to reclaim new hostnames are resolved but not cached (existing entries are still
served and refreshed) so memory cannot grow without limit.
.B 0
means unlimited. Default:
.BR 65536 .
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-nr\-dns\-workers=\fInr\fR
Number of DNS resolver worker threads kept running, at most 1024. This does not
cap how many lookups run at once: more threads are started on demand when lookups
are waiting, up to 64 threads or
.IR nr ,
whichever is larger, and those exit again after 10 seconds idle. Default:
.BR 4 .
.IP
Each thread runs one blocking lookup, so the threads are shared out by client
address, grouped as for
.BR \-\-max\-conns\-per\-ip :
an IPv4 address, or the /64 of an IPv6 one. A client runs at most half of the
threads at a time, clients with lookups waiting take turns, and a quarter of the
threads are kept for clients with no lookup running, who also go first, so slow
names from a client or two do not hold up the others. It takes about 18 client
addresses, at 64 threads, to use up the kept threads as well. A new client then
waits for the next thread to come free, up to as long as one lookup takes, and
longer with many more addresses than threads, as each of them gets a turn first.
At most 1024 lookups wait per client and 16384 in all; a request over either
limit is answered at once like a name
that does not resolve (SOCKS5 REP 0x04, HTTP 502). A request for a name already
being looked up shares that lookup, and no limit applies to it. When all clients
arrive from one address, as behind another proxy, they count as one client; a
larger
.I nr
raises its share.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-dns\-server=\fIaddr:port\fR
Upstream DNS server for the built\-in raw resolver (see
.BR \-\-raw\-dns ).
Requires a build with the new DNS resolver. Default:
.BR 1.1.1.1 .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-raw\-dns=\fI0|1\fR
Use the built\-in raw DNS resolver for SOCKS5/HTTP hostname targets instead of
the system resolver. An IP literal target is connected to as it is and never
sent to the DNS server. Requires a build with the new DNS resolver and the
.B epoll
event loop; combining it with
.B \-\-event\-loop=io_uring
or with
.B \-\-dns\-cache\-secs
is rejected at startup. It yields a single address per name, so multi\-address
fallback and racing do not apply (see
.BR "TARGET ADDRESS SELECTION" ).
Default:
.BR 0 .
.SS Upstream proxy chaining
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-upstream\-proxy=\fIurl\fR
Route every outgoing connection through a next\-hop proxy, but for those an ACL
.B "\-j UPSTREAM"
rule sends elsewhere (see
.BR "UPSTREAM CHAINING" ).
The URL scheme selects the proxy type:
.BR socks5://[user:pass@]host:port ,
a SOCKS5 proxy that resolves target hostnames locally;
.BR socks5h://[user:pass@]host:port ,
a SOCKS5 proxy that resolves them itself;
.BR http://[user:pass@]host:port ,
an HTTP proxy reached with an HTTP CONNECT request; or
.BR https://[user:pass@]host:port ,
the same over TLS, which needs a build with OpenSSL support (see
.B UPSTREAM CHAINING
for what is verified). The host may be a name or an IP address literal, an
IPv6 one in brackets. Optional
.B user:pass
supplies username/password authentication to the upstream (RFC\ 1929 for
SOCKS5, HTTP Basic for HTTP and HTTPS). The port defaults to 1080 for SOCKS5
and 8080 for HTTP and HTTPS.
gwproxy overwrites the password in its command line with
.B ***
as soon as it has read it, but other local users can still catch it there in
the moment before, and it stays in shell history and service definitions;
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy\-auth\-file
keeps it out of the command line altogether.
.TP
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy\-auth\-file=\fIfile\fR
Read the upstream credentials from the first line of
.I file
instead of the
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
URL, as
.B user:pass
(split at the first colon) or a bare
.BR user ,
each part at most 255 bytes. The command line is readable by every local
user (\fBps\fR(1), \fI/proc/<pid>/cmdline\fR), so this keeps the password
out of it; make the file readable only by the user gwproxy runs as. The
password may contain any character except a line break, including
.B @
and
.BR : .
The URL must not carry credentials of its own when this option is given.
.TP
.BR \-\-upstream\-ca\-file=\fIfile\fR
Verify the certificates of
.B https://
upstream proxies against the CA certificates in
.I file
(PEM, one or more) instead of the system's default trust store: of an
.B https://
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
and of the
.B https://
servers of the ACL file's upstream pools alike, one trust store for all.
Needs an
.B https://
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
or an
.BR \-\-acl\-file ;
it is read once, at startup, and a file that cannot be read or holds no
certificate is an error then, even when no pool has an
.B https://
server yet. Only in builds with OpenSSL support.
.SS Networking
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mark=\fInr\fR
Set
.B SO_MARK
(fwmark) on outgoing connections, for policy routing and iptables matching.
The mark is a 32\-bit value, written in decimal or, with a
.B 0x
prefix, in hexadecimal.
Requires
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
or, since Linux 5.11,
.BR CAP_NET_RAW .
.B 0
disables it. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-bind\-source=\fIaddr\fR [ :\fIport\fR ]
Pin every outgoing connection to this source address with
.BR bind (2).
This is the global default for the ACL's
.BR "\-j BIND \-\-to\-source" ,
and takes the same argument: an IP literal, never a hostname \(em an IPv4
address, a bare IPv6 address, or a bracketed
.RI [ v6 ]
when a
.I port
follows. The port is written in decimal digits only, 0 to 65535; anything else
is refused at startup. Omit the port (the default) for an ephemeral source
port, chosen per destination at
.BR connect (2);
pinning a fixed one limits that source to a single connection at a time and
defeats multi\-address racing, as described under
.BR \-\-acl\-file .
A source whose address family differs from the address being dialled is
.I skipped
rather than refused: an IPv4
.B \-\-bind\-source
leaves an IPv6 target on its default source, so setting one does not quietly
turn a dual\-stack proxy into a single\-family one. (An ACL
.B \-\-to\-source
that mismatches stays an error \(em an explicit rule naming the wrong family is
a mistake, a default that cannot apply is not.) An IPv4\-mapped address
.RI ( ::ffff:a.b.c.d )
is IPv4 for this comparison, as a target and as a source. Default: unset.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-bind\-iface=\fIname\fR
Pin every outgoing connection to this network interface
.RB ( SO_BINDTODEVICE ),
the global default for the ACL's
.BR "\-j BIND \-\-to\-iface" .
It has been unprivileged since Linux 5.7; older kernels wanted
.BR CAP_NET_RAW .
Only the length of
.I name
is validated at startup (at most 15 characters), not its existence, so an
interface that only appears later \(em a WireGuard or PPP link \(em is a valid
value; until it exists, every outgoing connection fails. Default: unset.
.IP
Both options are
.I strict:
if the bind fails, the connection is dropped rather than egressing from the
wrong source or out of the wrong interface, which is the leak they exist to
prevent. In this they differ from the global
.BR \-\-mark ,
which stays best\-effort. They apply to TCP target connections \(em SOCKS5,
HTTP, plain and transparent forwarding, and the hop to an
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
\(em but not to the SOCKS5 UDP relay, matching the reach of
.BR "\-j BIND" .
A matching
.B \-j BIND
rule
.I replaces
them wholesale for the connections it matches: a rule carrying only
.B \-\-to\-iface
does not inherit
.BR \-\-bind\-source ,
just as
.B \-j MARK
replaces rather than merges with
.BR \-\-mark .
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-target\-buf\-size=\fInr\fR
Size in bytes of the per\-connection buffer for target\(->client data. Default:
.BR 65536 .
At least 256 for SOCKS5 or HTTP, and at least 1280 with
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy ,
whose handshake goes through this buffer.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-client\-buf\-size=\fInr\fR
Size in bytes of the per\-connection buffer for client\(->target data. Default:
.BR 65536 .
At least 256 for HTTP, and at least 513 for SOCKS5, the size of its longest
request.
.IP
Each wakeup reads at most one buffer and writes it out, so the buffer size
sets the syscalls per byte of a bulk transfer: 64 KiB takes about 40%
less CPU per byte than 16 KiB on loopback, and larger sizes gain little
more. Each connection allocates both buffers, so the pair reserves
.B 128
KiB of address space; 10,000 concurrent connections reserve about
.B 1.2
GiB. Only the pages a connection has actually used count towards the
resident memory, so idle and short request/response connections stay at a
few KiB each. Lower the sizes if memory matters more than syscall count,
raise them for fewer, larger reads and writes.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-tcp\-nodelay=\fI0|1\fR
Enable
.B TCP_NODELAY
on connections. Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BR \-K ", " \-\-tcp\-quickack=\fI0|1\fR
Obsolete and ignored, with a warning. It never set
.BR TCP_QUICKACK ,
which the kernel clears again by itself, so it cannot be set once per
connection. It is still accepted so that existing command lines start.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-tcp\-keepalive=\fI0|1\fR
Enable
.B TCP
keepalive on connections. Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-tcp\-keepidle=\fIsec\fR
.B TCP_KEEPIDLE
in seconds. Default:
.BR 60 .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-tcp\-keepintvl=\fIsec\fR
.B TCP_KEEPINTVL
in seconds. Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-tcp\-keepcnt=\fInr\fR
.B TCP_KEEPCNT
probe count. Default:
.BR 5 .
.SS Logging
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-log\-level=\fIlevel\fR
Verbosity:
.B 0
none,
.B 1
error,
.B 2
warning,
.B 3
info,
.B 4
debug. Default:
.BR 3 .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-log\-file=\fIfile\fR
Write logs to
.IR file .
Default:
.BR /dev/stdout .
.SH PROXY MODES
.SS Plain TCP forwarding
With no proxy mode selected and
.B \-\-target
set, every accepted connection is spliced to the fixed target address. This is
a straight byte forwarder with no protocol handling.
.SS SOCKS5
.RB ( \-\-as\-socks5=1 )
Implements the SOCKS5 CONNECT command (RFC 1928) for IPv4, IPv6 and
domain\-name (ATYP) targets. With
.B \-\-auth\-file
set, the username/password method (RFC 1929) is required; otherwise the
no\-authentication method is offered.
.SS HTTP
.RB ( \-\-as\-http=1 )
Handles two request styles on the same port:
.IP \(bu 3
.B CONNECT
requests, tunnelling arbitrary TCP (typically end\-to\-end TLS) to the named
host and port;
.IP \(bu 3
.B forwarding
of ordinary absolute\-form requests such as "GET http://host/path", which
gwproxy rewrites to origin form, fetches from the origin and relays back, one
request per connection.
.PP
Only that one request reaches the origin: its body, delimited by Content\-Length
or chunked Transfer\-Encoding, is forwarded, and anything the client sends after
it is discarded. A request gwproxy cannot delimit unambiguously (both framing
headers, a Transfer\-Encoding other than "chunked", a Content\-Length that is
not plain digits or exceeds 9223372036854775807, the largest signed 64\-bit
integer, or a Connection header naming Content\-Length, Transfer\-Encoding or
Host) is answered with "400 Bad Request". So is a malformed chunked body, including a
chunk extension off the grammar of RFC 9112 Section 7.1.1 (an empty extension,
an unterminated quoted string, whitespace before the line end), when it comes
with the header; one that turns out malformed later closes the connection.
A field value may hold bytes 0x80 to 0xff, which are passed on unchanged, and
none of them makes a framing header acceptable; any other control byte than a
tab in a value closes the connection without a reply.
.PP
A field that may appear only once (Authorization, Content\-Length,
Content\-Location, Content\-Range, Content\-Type, Cookie, Date, Expect, From,
Host, If\-Modified\-Since, If\-Range, If\-Unmodified\-Since, Max\-Forwards, Origin,
Proxy\-Authorization, Referer or User\-Agent) is answered with "400 Bad Request"
if it is repeated, on a CONNECT as well. The usual list fields, such as Accept or
Cache\-Control, have their repeated lines joined with ", "; any other repeated
field, such as X\-Forwarded\-For, keeps its separate lines, in their order. As
with every field, each line reaches the origin spelled "Name: value", and a
hop\-by\-hop field or one that Connection names is dropped.
.PP
A forwarding request with a field name that reads, in any case and with each
"_" taken for "\-", as Content\-Length, Transfer\-Encoding, Host or a hop\-by\-hop
field (Connection, Proxy\-Connection, Keep\-Alive, TE, Trailer, Upgrade,
Proxy\-Authorization, Proxy\-Authenticate), such as Content_Length, is answered
with "400 Bad Request": an origin that maps "_" and "\-" to the same name, as CGI
does, could read it as the field itself. Other names with underscores are
forwarded unchanged.
.PP
A "Proxy" field, in any case, is dropped from a forwarding request as well,
though it is no hop\-by\-hop field: an origin that runs CGI programs would hand
it to them as HTTP_PROXY, which many HTTP libraries take for the proxy of their
own outgoing requests ("httpoxy"), so the client could pick where the origin's
requests go. No other name reaches CGI as HTTP_PROXY, so it has no alias to
refuse.
.PP
The origin's response header is rewritten before the client sees it: its
hop\-by\-hop fields are dropped and "Connection: close" is added, so the client
does not send another request on the connection, and the connection is closed
once the origin has finished. A response that is not valid HTTP/1.x, one whose
header is too large, and a 407 from the origin, which would invite the client
to hand over its proxy credentials, are answered with "502 Bad Gateway"
instead. The response header, together with any interim (1xx) responses before
it that the client has not yet received, may take at most
.B \-\-target\-buf\-size
minus 64 bytes, and must still fit in
.B \-\-target\-buf\-size
once rewritten (bare LF line ends become CRLF).
.PP
A request header may take at most 16384 bytes, and must fit in
.B \-\-client\-buf\-size
as a whole; a larger one, or one with more than 128 field lines, is answered
with "431 Request Header Fields Too Large".
.PP
With
.B \-\-auth\-file
set, "Basic" proxy authentication (RFC 7617) is required and a missing or wrong
credential is answered with "407 Proxy Authentication Required".
.SS Combined SOCKS5 + HTTP
Setting both
.B \-\-as\-socks5=1
and
.B \-\-as\-http=1
serves both protocols on the same listening port. The protocol is detected from
the client's first bytes, and both share the same credential store.
.SH TLS TERMINATION
When built with OpenSSL and started with
.B \-\-tls\-cert
and
.BR \-\-tls\-key ,
gwproxy can terminate a client's TLS session on its own listener (an "HTTPS
proxy"), then run the ordinary SOCKS5/HTTP logic on the decrypted stream. This
encrypts the client\(->proxy hop, protecting proxy credentials and, for cleartext
.B http://
targets, the request and URL.
.PP
TLS is applied per connection by auto\-detecting the first byte: a value of
.B 0x16
(a TLS ClientHello) starts a handshake, while SOCKS5, SOCKS4 and HTTP first
bytes do not, so
.B plaintext SOCKS5/HTTP clients keep working unchanged on the same port.
gwproxy advertises only the "http/1.1" ALPN protocol, cleanly downgrading an
HTTP/2\-preferring client. The OpenSSL engine is driven entirely through memory
BIOs and never touches the socket directly. TLS termination works on both the
epoll and io_uring event loops.
.PP
The end of a TLS client's stream reaches the target as a normal end of stream
only if it is one. If a record fails to verify, the client sends a fatal alert,
its connection is reset, its TCP stream stops in the middle of a record, or
gwproxy itself gives up on the connection or shuts down before the client
finished sending, the target's connection is reset (TCP RST) rather than closed,
so that a protocol delimited by the connection closing does not take the stream
for complete. A reset drops what the target's socket had not sent yet, which is
no loss to a stream that was cut anyway. A TCP FIN after a whole record with no
.B close_notify
is still passed on as a normal end of stream: many clients close that way, and
gwproxy cannot tell them from an attacker who injects the FIN at that point.
.PP
This is distinct from an HTTP
.B CONNECT
tunnel to an
.B https://
origin, which gwproxy blind\-tunnels end\-to\-end without terminating TLS, and
which needs no certificate.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
The credential file named by
.B \-\-auth\-file
holds one
.B username:password
entry per line. The same store backs SOCKS5 (RFC 1929) and HTTP "Basic" (RFC
7617) authentication; whenever it is configured, clients of either protocol
must authenticate. Credentials can be updated without a restart: edit the file
and send gwproxy a
.B SIGHUP
(see
.BR "RELOADING THE FILES" ).
.SH RELOADING THE FILES
.B SIGHUP
reloads
.B \-\-auth\-file
and the
.B \-\-acl\-file
with every file it includes, all at once and with no further wait: sending it
says the files are ready. Each file that fails to load (missing, unreadable,
malformed) is logged with the reason and keeps its current contents in force,
while the other one is still reloaded; each outcome is logged
.RB ( "Reloaded ACL file" ,
.BR "Failed to reload authentication file" ,
\&...). A
.B SIGHUP
with neither file configured is logged as having nothing to reload. Signals
that arrive while a reload runs give one more reload once it is over, not one
each. The reload runs on a thread of its own, so the workers go on serving
throughout.
.PP
An ACL reload rebuilds the upstream pools too (see
.BR "UPSTREAM CHAINING" ):
every pool server is parsed and its host resolved again, and a server that
fails fails the reload, which keeps the rules and pools in force. New
connections use the new pools; one already through a pool's server keeps that
server until it ends. The servers' health, an ejection included, carries over
as their counts do.
.PP
The auth file, like the ACL files, must be a regular file. A FIFO or a device
put in its place is refused, not waited on: the reload
.RB ( SIGHUP ,
or a change seen by the watch) fails at once, says so, and keeps the current
credentials in force. A reload of a very large file still takes as long as
reading it, and meanwhile no other reload runs, and a
.B SIGINT
or
.B SIGTERM
stops the workers but the process does not exit until that reload is over.
.PP
By default nothing else reloads the files: editing one has no effect until the
next
.BR SIGHUP .
With
.BR \-\-watch\-files=1 ,
gwproxy also watches them and reloads a file by itself when it changes. A file
written through the configured name in its directory is reloaded as soon as the
writer closes it, and a file renamed to that name at once. Any other change to
the file the path leads to (behind a symlink, a Kubernetes secret mount, a
swapped directory, unlink and link, or a change of its mode or owner) is seen by
a
.BR stat (2)
of the path every second, and is reloaded once the file has not changed for 5
seconds, so a revoked credential stops working about 6 seconds later. That wait
is what keeps a writer's pause from being taken for the end of its work, but a
writer that pauses for longer can still have a half written file reloaded: a
truncated file then refuses every client, and a partial
.B \-\-acl\-file
can allow what the finished one rejects. Replace these files atomically (write a
temporary file in the same directory and rename it over the old one) if that
matters. A file that disappears or cannot be read is logged once and the current
contents stay in force until it comes back, and a reload that fails for want of
descriptors or memory is retried every second. A FIFO or device found this way
is logged once and not read.
.SH TRANSPARENT PROXY
.RB ( \-\-as\-transparent=1 )
Transparent mode takes each connection's original destination from the
.B SO_ORIGINAL_DST
socket option, so it must sit behind an iptables
.B REDIRECT
rule. To avoid a redirect loop, gwproxy's own outgoing connections can be marked
(with
.BR \-\-mark )
and excluded from the REDIRECT:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind=127.0.0.1:1080 \-\-as\-transparent=1 \-\-mark=1
iptables \-t nat \-A OUTPUT \-p tcp \-\-dport 80 \-m mark ! \-\-mark 1 \\
    \-j REDIRECT \-\-to\-ports 1080
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B \-\-mark
requires
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
or
.BR CAP_NET_RAW ,
and installing the iptables rules requires the corresponding privileges;
both typically need root.
.SH UPSTREAM CHAINING
With
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy ,
every outgoing connection is made through a next\-hop proxy rather than
directly. A
.B socks5://
upstream resolves target hostnames locally before connecting, while
.B socks5h://
defers name resolution to the upstream proxy. An
.B http://
upstream is reached with an HTTP CONNECT request, which likewise resolves the
host at the proxy. Credentials embedded in the URL authenticate to the upstream.
In transparent mode the
upstream, of any type, is asked for the connection's original destination
address.
.PP
Plain
.B \-\-target
forwarding through a
.BR socks5h:// " or " http://
upstream goes through the
.B OUTPUT
chain like any other, the built\-in default ACL included. An IP literal target
is taken as an address:
.B \-d
rules match it,
.B \-j DNAT
rewrites it, and the upstream is asked for that address. A hostname is not
resolved here: the chain sees it as with any other remote\-DNS request
.RB ( \-m " domain matches, " \-d " does not), and it is handed to the upstream"
unless a
.B \-j DNAT
to an address replaces it. A
.B \-\-target
that is not a valid hostname or address with a port is refused at startup.
.PP
An
.B https://
upstream is an HTTP proxy reached over TLS: the HTTP CONNECT request, its
credentials and the tunnel behind it all go through the TLS session, on
both event loops. Nothing is sent to the proxy before the TLS handshake has
completed and its certificate has been verified. The certificate chain must
lead to a CA in the system's default trust store (OpenSSL's, which honours
the
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
and
.B SSL_CERT_DIR
environment variables) or, with
.BR \-\-upstream\-ca\-file ,
to one in that file, and the certificate must be valid at the time. It must
also be the URL's host's: a name must be among the certificate's DNS names
(a wildcard covers one whole label at most; the subject's common name does
not count), and is sent as the TLS server
name (SNI); an IP address literal must be among its IP addresses, and is not
sent as SNI. TLS 1.2 is the oldest version accepted. A handshake that fails,
or a certificate that is refused, fails the connection as any other upstream
failure does (SOCKS5 reply 0x01, HTTP 502 to the client, after a pool's retry
on another server; see
.BR "Upstream pools" )
and is logged with the reason, for example
.IR "certificate verify failed: hostname mismatch" .
.PP
An upstream that turns the CONNECT down is answered to an HTTP client with
"502 Bad Gateway", whatever its reason. A SOCKS5 client gets reply 0x05
(connection refused) only when the upstream says the target could not be
reached: a
.B socks5://
or
.B socks5h://
upstream's failed reply, or a 502 or 504 from an
.B http://
or
.B https://
one. A 407, which says the upstream refused gwproxy's credentials, and any
other status (403, 500, 503, ...) say nothing about the target and get reply
0x01 (general failure), as does a SOCKS5 upstream that refuses the
credentials.
.PP
The end of the upstream's stream reaches the client as a normal end of stream
only if it is one, as for a TLS client in the other direction (see
.BR "TLS TERMINATION" ).
Once the tunnel through an
.B https://
upstream is up, the client's connection is reset (TCP RST) rather than closed
whenever the pair goes down before the client's side was shut after a whole
stream: when the upstream's stream is cut (a record that fails to verify, a
fatal alert, a reset, a stream that stops in the middle of a record), and
equally on any other teardown, including
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
and gwproxy shutting down. A tunnel that fails before it is up, and a 502
gwproxy sends in place of an unusable response to a forwarded request, end
with a normal close. When the client is done sending, the upstream is sent a
.B close_notify
before the TCP FIN. A TLS client
.RB ( \-\-tls\-cert )
may chain through an
.B https://
upstream; each side has its own TLS session.
.SS Upstream pools
The ACL file
.RB ( \-\-acl\-file )
can send connections through other upstreams than
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy ,
per user, client, port or target: an
.BI "upstream " name " {"
block defines a pool of upstream proxies, and an
.B OUTPUT
rule with
.BI "\-j UPSTREAM \-\-to " name
sends the connections it matches through it, or with
.B "\-j UPSTREAM \-\-to DIRECT"
to their targets directly. Of several matching
.B \-j UPSTREAM
rules the last one decides, unless one with
.B \-\-accept
ends the walk first; with none,
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
applies, or no upstream at all. For example:
.PP
.RS
.EX
upstream office {
    server socks5h://alice_up:secret@10.0.0.1:1080;
}
upstream web {
    server http://10.0.0.3:3128;
}
\-A OUTPUT \-m user \-\-user alice \-j UPSTREAM \-\-to office
\-A OUTPUT \-m user \-\-user bob \-j UPSTREAM \-\-to web
\-A OUTPUT \-m domain \-\-domain intranet.example \-j UPSTREAM \-\-to DIRECT
.EE
.RE
.PP
A pool's
.B https://
servers are verified as an
.B https://
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
is (see above), against the same trust store, the system's or
.BR \-\-upstream\-ca\-file ,
each against its own host, which is sent as SNI when it is a name. A pool
may mix
.B https://
servers with
.BR http:// " and " socks5h://
ones. An
.B https://
server may come in with a reload into a proxy that had none, and one whose
host cannot be used as a TLS server name fails the load like any bad server.
.PP
The upstream is decided once per connection, before its target's name is
resolved, from what is known then: the user, the client's address and port,
and the target as the client gave it, a name or an address, and its port.
So
.B \-m domain
matches a target given by name, and
.B \-d
only one given as an address (a name's addresses are not known yet). For a
target given by name the decision passes over every rule with a
.BR \-d ,
terminal ones included: a
.B "\-d ... \-j ACCEPT"
above a
.B \-j UPSTREAM
rule does not stop a named target from picking that rule's pool, though it
may accept the target's addresses afterwards. The decision is not the verdict
either: it takes the choice of the last
.B \-j UPSTREAM
rule it meets, whether the walk then ends on an accept or a reject, so a
default\-deny set that admits targets by
.B \-d
still sends them through their pool. A
.B \-m statistic
rule takes no turn in this decision, it tells what the connection's next
evaluation will. A regexp that cannot be decided (one whose match runs into
PCRE2's limits, which refuses the target in any evaluation)
refuses the connection rather than leave it to the default. The
.B OUTPUT
evaluations that follow, once per resolved address and again when a connect is
retried, decide the verdict,
.BR DNAT ", " MARK " and " BIND
as before, but never change the upstream.
.PP
The pool then decides how the name is resolved: a
.B socks5://
pool, like
.B DIRECT
and no upstream at all, has gwproxy resolve it and sends the server an
address; a
.BR socks5h:// ", " http:// " or " https://
pool sends the server the name.
Plain
.B \-\-target
forwarding to a name sends that name to a pool that resolves names. A
connection the ACL sends elsewhere goes to the address the name resolved to
at startup; when
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
itself resolves names remotely, the name is not resolved at startup, and such
a connection is refused (and logged).
.PP
A pool of several servers spreads its connections over them. By default it
takes them in turn (round\-robin): each connection that picks the pool goes
to the next server, in the order the block lists them, whichever worker
serves it. With
.BR least_conn ,
a connection goes to the server with the fewest connections open through it
at that moment, and among servers equally loaded to the next one in turn, so
that they share the new connections. A connection counts against its server
from the moment it is given it, before it connects, until it ends, however it
ends: closed by either side, refused, failed to connect or in the handshake,
or timed out. The counts belong to the server, not to the pool: a server,
known by its scheme, the address it resolved to, its port and its user (not
its password), counts the connections of every pool that lists it, and keeps
its count across a reload, so connections opened before a reload still weigh
on the choices made after it. A reload starts each pool's turn from its first
server again. Workers choosing at the same moment may see the same counts and
choose the same server; the choices after them see the result.
.PP
Round\-robin costs the same whatever the size of the pool: a connection looks
only at the server whose turn it is (and, when that one is ejected, at a few
more, see below).
.B least_conn
has to compare the counts of all the servers: each new connection reads the
count of every server of its pool, stopping early only at a server with none
open, so in a pool of thousands of busy servers every new connection costs a
scan of thousands of counts that other workers keep changing. Prefer
round\-robin for large pools unless the load must follow the counts.
.PP
A pool also keeps track of its servers' health, from the connections they
carry (passively: gwproxy sends no probes of its own). A failure counts
against a server only when it is the server's own: its TCP connection
refused, reset or timed out, its TLS handshake failing (a refused
certificate included), a SOCKS5 server that accepts none of the offered
authentication methods, refuses the credentials or answers reply 0x07 or
0x08, an HTTP server that answers 407, a reply that does not parse, the
server hanging up, or the connect timeout running out before gwproxy has
sent its CONNECT request (during the TCP connect, the TLS handshake or the
SOCKS5 method and authentication exchange). A failure the server reports for
the target (SOCKS5 replies 0x03 to 0x06, HTTP 502 and 504) reaches the client
as it is and never counts, and neither do ambiguous refusals (SOCKS5 replies
0x01 and 0x02, HTTP 403, 500, 503 and any other status), the timeout running
out while the server is waiting for the target to answer the CONNECT (a
target that never answers holds a healthy server up), nor local trouble such
as running out of descriptors. Three failures
that count, within 30 seconds, eject the server for 5 minutes: no pool picks
it meanwhile. When its turn comes, round\-robin tries up to 4 servers of
the pool at random and takes the first one in service, so the turns of
ejected servers, even of a run of them, are spread evenly over the servers
left instead of all landing on the next one; only if all 4 tries find an
ejected server does it take the next server in service in the block's
order.
.B least_conn
chooses among the servers left. As with the counts, the health
is the server's, not the pool's: failures through one pool eject the server
from every pool that lists it. When every server of a pool is ejected, the
pool takes the one whose ejection ends soonest; a pool of one server, and
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy ,
keep using theirs, so for them an ejection shows only in the log. A server
that fails while ejected is not ejected for longer; once its 5 minutes are
over it is picked again, and if it failed in its last 30 seconds out, its
next failure ejects it again at once. A connection that gets through does not
undo the failures before it: they count until they are 30 seconds old. These
numbers are fixed.
.PP
A connection that a pool's server fails in one of the ways that count is
tried again, once, on another server of the same pool that is not ejected:
the next one in the block's order (when that one is ejected, one found as
for an ejected server's turn), or with
.B least_conn
the least loaded one. This is safe because nothing the client sends is
passed on before the tunnel through the upstream is up, so the retry repeats
nothing and loses nothing. It has what is left of the connection's
.BR \-\-connect\-timeout ,
no more: a connection that ran out of time is not retried, and when the retry
runs out of it, that does not count against its server. With no other
server to take, or once its retry has failed too, the client gets the reply
it would have had without one: SOCKS5 reply 0x05 for a refused connection and
0x01 for a failed handshake, or HTTP 502. A failure that does not count is not
retried, and neither is a connection through
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy .
.PP
A reload keeps what is known of each server that the new pools still list,
ejection included, as it keeps the counts. A server that the reload takes out
of every pool is remembered only while connections opened before it still use
that server; once they are gone it is forgotten, and a later reload that
lists it again starts it afresh.
.PP
An ejection is logged at warning level, once however many pools list the
server, as
.IR "Upstream pool NAME server N (ADDRESS) ejected for 300 s after 3 failures in 30 s" ,
naming the pool of the connection whose failure ejected it (or
.I default upstream (ADDRESS)
for
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy ),
and the server's return at info level, when a connection first finds the 5
minutes over. At debug level each failure is logged with whether it counted,
each retry with both servers, and each connection that had to take an ejected
server.
.PP
Each connection that a rule sends through a pool, or
.BR DIRECT ,
is logged at debug level, naming the pool and its server's number, counted
from 1, how many connections that server then has open, and its address,
never its credentials; so is each connection giving its server back, with
the count left. A pool server's address is
resolved once, when the set loads; a reload resolves it again. A reload that
changes the pools applies to new connections; one already through a pool's
server keeps it until it ends.
.PP
UDP is never sent through an upstream, and the rules do not apply to the UDP
relay's datagrams. A SOCKS5 UDP ASSOCIATE is decided like a connection, from
the user and the client alone, as for a TCP connection. It has no target, so
a rule that tests one
.RB ( \-d ", " \-\-dports " or " "\-m domain" ,
negated or not) is never taken for it, a terminal one such as
.B "\-d ... \-j ACCEPT"
included. A user the rules send through a pool is refused it (reply 0x07), and
so is one that a pool rule
.RB ( "\-p tcp" ,
or with no protocol) would match but for its target tests, as the rule might
send that user's traffic through the pool; a
.B \-m statistic
in such a rule counts as one that could match, and a later
.B DIRECT
rule that does match does not undo the refusal. A
.B DIRECT
rule passed over for its target tests is just passed over. Everyone else
without a choice is refused it when
.B \-\-upstream\-proxy
is given, and a user the rules send
.B DIRECT
gets a direct UDP relay, even past
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy .
.SH TARGET ADDRESS SELECTION
A hostname target of a SOCKS5 or HTTP request usually resolves to several
addresses, and gwproxy tries them as a list of candidates rather than
committing to one. Only names gwproxy resolves itself get such a list: a
literal IPv4/IPv6 target, plain
.B \-\-target
forwarding and transparent mode each have exactly one destination, the SOCKS5
UDP relay takes a literal address from every datagram (a domain\-name datagram
is dropped), and a hostname given to
.B \-\-target
is resolved once at startup, to a single address \(em or, with a
.BR socks5h:// " or " http://
upstream, not resolved here at all but handed to the proxy.
.PP
The candidates are interleaved by address family \(em the preferred family
first, then the two families alternating. With the default
.B \-\-prefer\-ipv6=0
that is the first IPv4 address, the first IPv6 address, the second IPv4
address, and so on; with
.B \-\-prefer\-ipv6=1
the order is mirrored. When one family runs out, the remaining addresses of the
other follow in order. Within a family the resolver's own order is preserved,
so the system resolver's RFC 6724 sorting still decides which address of that
family comes first; only its cross\-family part is overridden. Alternating is
what makes a family that is broken end to end (a black\-holed IPv6 route is the
usual case) cost one failed attempt rather than one attempt per address. The
first candidate is the address a single\-shot selection would have picked, so
the first attempt is unchanged.
.PP
One connection tries at most the first
.B 4
addresses of a name \(em a longer answer is truncated without a diagnostic \(em
so at most four attempts race. Names are resolved with
.BR AI_ADDRCONFIG ,
so a family the host has no address configured for contributes no candidates at
all.
.PP
gwproxy starts the first candidate immediately. If it has neither succeeded nor
failed within
.BR \-\-connect\-attempt\-delay ,
the next candidate is started alongside it (Happy Eyeballs, RFC 8305 Section
5); the first connection to succeed wins and the others are closed. An attempt
that fails while another is still in flight does not start a replacement at
that moment \(em the attempt still running may yet succeed, and a
slow\-but\-working address should not be abandoned because a faster one was
refused \(em so the next candidate joins on the following delay expiry instead.
The delay is nominal spacing rather than a guaranteed minimum: an address that
fails while it is the only attempt in flight is stepped over at once, and the
candidate after it can then follow in less than a full delay.
.PP
.B \-\-connect\-timeout
bounds the whole operation across every candidate. It is armed once, when the
first attempt starts, and racing does not extend it, so a name whose addresses
are all dead still fails after one connect timeout rather than four. With
.B \-\-connect\-attempt\-delay=0
(or a negative value) at most one attempt is in flight; sequential fallback
still steps to the next candidate whenever one actively fails, but a
black\-holed address is never stepped over \(em the connection dies at
.B \-\-connect\-timeout
(SOCKS5 reply
.BR 0x06 ,
HTTP
.BR 504 )
with the remaining candidates untried.
.PP
The
.B OUTPUT
chain (see
.BR \-\-acl\-file )
is evaluated once per candidate, not once per name, so only the address\-based
match
.RB ( "\-d / \-\-destination" )
can differ between the candidates of one request; the others
.RB ( "\-s / \-\-source" ", " "\-m domain" ", " "\-m user" ", "
.BR "\-p / \-\-protocol" ", " \-\-sports " and " \-\-dports )
see the same values for all of them.
.BR "\-j DNAT" ", " "\-j MARK" " and " "\-j BIND"
are likewise applied per candidate, and a
.B \-j DNAT
matching every candidate of a name collapses the race onto one destination,
which is then dialled once per candidate. A candidate the chain denies is
skipped rather than being fatal, as is one rejected by a strict
.BR "\-j BIND" " or " "\-j MARK" ,
or by the global
.BR \-\-bind\-source / \-\-bind\-iface ;
the request fails only once every candidate has been skipped or has failed to
connect.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy ,
racing is disabled. A
.B socks5://
upstream resolves target hostnames locally, so a candidate list still exists
there: it is used for the per\-candidate
.B OUTPUT
evaluation and for the destination named to the upstream, and a failed connect
to the proxy is retried once per remaining candidate before the client is told.
A
.BR socks5h:// " or " http://
upstream resolves the name itself, so no candidate list is built and exactly
one connect is made.
.PP
Multi\-address fallback and racing work on both the
.B epoll
and the
.B io_uring
event loop. A racing connection holds one target socket per attempt in flight,
up to four instead of one, and on
.B epoll
it additionally holds a file descriptor for its attempt timer, which the
.B io_uring
loop does not need; size the descriptor limit accordingly, or set
.B \-\-connect\-attempt\-delay=0
to keep a single attempt in flight.
.PP
.B \-\-raw\-dns
defeats all of this: the built\-in raw resolver yields a single address per name
(it queries one record type at a time and tries the other only when the first
yields nothing), so there is nothing to fall back to and nothing to race.
.SH EVENT LOOPS
Each worker uses one event loop, selected with
.BR \-\-event\-loop .
.B epoll
is always available and is the default.
.B io_uring
is available only in builds configured with io_uring support and is selected at
run time. All proxy features, including TLS termination and the multi\-address
racing described under
.BR "TARGET ADDRESS SELECTION" ,
work on both loops, except the built\-in raw DNS resolver
.RB ( \-\-raw\-dns ),
which requires
.BR epoll .
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Stop at once: workers leave their event loops and every connection is closed,
with whatever is still in its buffers or on its way discarded. There is no
drain. A client in the middle of a transfer sees a normal end of stream after
only part of the data, so a protocol that does not mark its own end cannot tell
this from a complete transfer. The process exits once a reload in progress is
over (see
.BR "RELOADING THE FILES" ).
Early in the startup, while it loads its files, the signal ends the process
at once. From when it starts its threads and opens its listeners until its
handler is in place, the signal is held instead, and then stops the proxy as
above, with exit status 0.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload
.B \-\-auth\-file
and the
.B \-\-acl\-file
set (see
.BR "RELOADING THE FILES" ).
The proxy goes on running whatever the outcome, and with neither file
configured the signal is only logged: a stray
.B SIGHUP
(a closed terminal, say) does not terminate the process.
.TP
.B SIGPIPE
Ignored, so a peer closing a connection cannot terminate the process.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B gwproxy
exits
.B 0
on a clean shutdown and non\-zero if the configuration is invalid or a fatal
runtime error occurs. The non\-zero status is an
.BR errno (3)
value, such as 22
.RB ( EINVAL )
for an invalid option, and the log says what failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I auth\-file
Line\-oriented
.B username:password
credential store (see
.BR \-\-auth\-file ).
.TP
.I pid\-file
Process\-ID file written when
.B \-\-pid\-file
is given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Plain TCP forwarder,
.BR [::]:8080 " \(-> " 127.0.0.1:80 :
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:8080' \-\-target=127.0.0.1:80
.EE
.RE
.PP
SOCKS5 proxy with username/password authentication:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \-\-auth\-file=auth.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
HTTP proxy (CONNECT and forwarding) on port 8080:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:8080' \-\-as\-http=1
.EE
.RE
.PP
SOCKS5 and HTTP on the same port, sharing one credential file:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \-\-as\-http=1 \-\-auth\-file=auth.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
HTTPS proxy terminating client TLS (plaintext clients still work on the same
port):
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:8080' \-\-as\-http=1 \-\-as\-socks5=1 \\
    \-\-tls\-cert=cert.pem \-\-tls\-key=key.pem
curl \-x https://127.0.0.1:8080 http://example.com/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Chain every outgoing connection through an upstream proxy (SOCKS5 or HTTP):
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \\
    \-\-upstream\-proxy=socks5h://user:pass@1.2.3.4:1080
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same through an HTTPS proxy, whose certificate a private CA issued:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \\
    \-\-upstream\-proxy=https://user:pass@proxy.example.net:3129 \\
    \-\-upstream\-ca\-file=/etc/gwproxy/proxy\-ca.pem
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the io_uring event loop and set SO_MARK 100 on outgoing packets:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \-\-event\-loop=io_uring \-\-mark=100
.EE
.RE
.PP
Send all egress out of a WireGuard interface, from that interface's address,
while one customer's traffic (matched by the address it comes from) leaves via
the second tunnel instead \(em the rule replaces both defaults at once:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \-\-acl\-file=acl.txt \\
    \-\-bind\-iface=wg0 \-\-bind\-source=10.7.0.2

$ cat acl.txt
\-A OUTPUT \-s 192.168.9.0/24 \-j BIND \-\-to\-iface wg1 \-\-to\-source 10.8.0.2
\-P OUTPUT ACCEPT
.EE
.RE
.SH AVAILABILITY
Some features are enabled at build time by
.BR ./configure :
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-use\-io\-uring
builds the io_uring event loop, then selectable with
.BR \-\-event\-loop=io_uring .
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-use\-openssl
builds TLS termination; without it the
.B \-\-tls\-cert
and
.B \-\-tls\-key
options are absent.
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-use\-new\-dns\-resolver
builds the raw DNS resolver, enabling
.B \-\-raw\-dns
and
.BR \-\-dns\-server .
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-use\-pcre
builds PCRE2 regexp matches for the ACL, enabling
.B \-\-domain\-regexp
and
.BR \-\-user\-regexp ;
without it those rule options are rejected.
.PP
Options for features not compiled in are rejected. Run
.B ./configure \-\-help
for the full list.
.SH NOTES
.B \-\-mark
requires
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
or, since Linux 5.11,
.BR CAP_NET_RAW .
.B \-\-bind\-iface
.RB ( SO_BINDTODEVICE )
needs no privilege on Linux 5.7 and later; older kernels required
.BR CAP_NET_RAW .
.B \-\-bind\-source
needs none for an address that is configured locally.
Transparent mode requires iptables REDIRECT rules, which typically need root.
Binding to a privileged port (< 1024) requires
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
or root.
